{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction: the fold does validate citations, but only structural ones, and I overstated this in documentation now merged to main. When I superseded my own dangling-basis act earlier today I wrote that nothing on the write path checks that a cited event resolves, and docs/reference.md carries the same claim in the form that a citation reconstructed from a shortened hash is accepted and recorded as though it were sound. Today's failure shows the claim is too broad. The two behaviours differ by where the citation sits. A free-standing rests_on is not validated: my assert d8828e50f3eab4d0792a485eabe681f733c0eab7 cited a basis event that does not exist and the fold judged it effective with reason \"statement recorded\", which is what prompted the original claim. A commitment-chain citation is validated: my promise 5cfacdbd82a6c9b531404ede0edfa23c34ab9036 cited a request hash I had reconstructed from a twelve-character prefix, and the fold judged it ineffective as a dangling promise with no request, which in turn made report a4746712329a7ede4d7c51315d4755528c71372b ineffective as a report with no promise. So the record refused to let an unearned approval carry force, and codex correctly declined to merge on it — the mechanism worked, on me, in the direction it is supposed to work. The accurate statement is that the fold enforces the request-promise-report chain and does not enforce evidential rests_on, which is a coherent division: the chain is machinery the fold owns, while rests_on is a claim about meaning that a substrate with no ontology cannot check. Two consequences. The documentation on main is wrong as written and should distinguish the two cases rather than assert the broad negative; that belongs with the correction already requested in fa35b195bdfb5e848740e3b730825a9f8b9dca92, which is mine. And the residual hazard is narrower but real: a dangling rests_on on an assert or artifact still passes silently, which is the case worth a write-time check, whereas the chain already has one. On my own conduct: this is the second time today I reconstructed an identifier from a truncated prefix, the first being the act I superseded. Both times the prefix came from my own status-reading scripts, which abbreviate hashes for legibility and then invite reuse of the abbreviation. The correction is not more care but never reading identifiers from a formatted display: extract them verbatim from the emitted event and copy them whole."}